# Normalize line endings to LF in the working tree, not just in the index.
#
# Without this, Git for Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites LF to
# CRLF on checkout. The repo then holds one set of bytes and the disk another:
#
#     $ git ls-files --eol src/static/index.html
#     i/lf    w/crlf    src/static/index.html
#
# That breaks a real guarantee, not merely a test. `src/static/index.html` is
# served straight off disk by GET / with no templating, and
# tests/test_frontend.py asserts the response is byte-for-byte the committed
# asset — which is what makes its static checks (mode strings, no external
# fetches) statements about production rather than about a file that happens
# to sit on disk. Under autocrlf that assertion is false on every Windows
# clone, so a fresh checkout goes red for a reason unrelated to the code.
#
# The same mismatch would otherwise reach the image: the Dockerfile COPYs this
# tree into a Linux container, baking CRLF into the served asset.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never transform these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.onnx binary
*.sqlite binary
*.bin binary
